; TEST: expect-exit=53
;
; The PARALLEL in parallel copy. The header's first two phis name each other
; on the back edge (`a = phi [b2: b]`, `b = phi [b2: a]`), so every trip round
; the loop swaps them; three trips from a=3, b=5 leave a=5, b=3, and the exit
; is a*10 + b = 53. Every wrong copy gives a different number:
;
;   correct parallel copy (read all, then write)    53
;   sequential `mov`s in phi order (a=b; b=a)       55   both become 5
;   an operand lookup that read the phi's own slot  35   nothing swaps
;   push in phi order, pop in the SAME order         5   the pops reverse
;                                                        the operands, so a
;                                                        takes k's 0 and k
;                                                        takes a's 3: the
;                                                        loop never runs
;
; THE PHI ORDER AND THE TRIP COUNT ARE LOAD-BEARING. The first version of
; this fixture put the counter phi first and ran three trips, and the
; same-order-pop mutation still exited with the right answer -- a pop order
; that reverses three operands happened to compose back to the correct swap
; after the entry edge and three back edges. Found by running the mutation,
; not by reading the fixture; the arrangement above was chosen so that each
; row of the table differs, and each was run.
;
; `-> u64` for tier1_combo_lt.ir's reason.

functio @initium (ptr) -> u64 {
b0:
%0 = param ptr 0
%1 = iconst u64 3
%2 = iconst u64 5
%3 = iconst u64 0
%4 = iconst u64 3
jmp b1
b1:
%5 = phi u64 b0 %1 b2 %6
%6 = phi u64 b0 %2 b2 %5
%7 = phi u64 b0 %3 b2 %9
%8 = cmp.lt u64 %7 %4
br %8 b2 b3
b2:
%9 = addw u64 %7 1
jmp b1
b3:
%10 = mulw u64 %5 10
%11 = addw u64 %10 %6
ret %11
}
